1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,109
This is RSI4GPF: a crew of AI agents and one coach AI, with recursive self-improvement, for GPU price forecasting.

2
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:13,315
We forecast thirty-seven GPU rental prices, one, three and seven days ahead.

3
00:00:13,315 --> 00:00:18,589
And we test which kinds of AI really help, on data the system has never seen.

4
00:00:19,339 --> 00:00:21,302
Here's the thing about these prices.

5
00:00:21,302 --> 00:00:23,920
Each one is just the middle of a list of offers.

6
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:25,338
So we asked two questions.

7
00:00:25,338 --> 00:00:28,829
Does looking at the whole list beat looking at the median alone?

8
00:00:28,829 --> 00:00:31,883
And which kinds of AI help actually survive a fair test?

9
00:00:32,633 --> 00:00:34,242
Let's start with the answer.

10
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:40,909
The forecast we hand in made twenty percent less error than drift, the standard baseline, on days no model had seen.

11
00:00:40,909 --> 00:00:46,081
That's every forecaster, scored on the same four hundred and seventy-nine fresh forecasts.

12
00:00:46,831 --> 00:00:47,814
Why does that work?

13
00:00:47,814 --> 00:00:52,778
Because two thirds of the movement in a published median isn't providers changing prices at all.

14
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:54,847
It's offers joining or leaving the list.

15
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:57,327
So our engine looks underneath.

16
00:00:57,327 --> 00:01:03,020
It replays what each offer did on past days, applies that to today's list, and takes the middle again.

17
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:05,810
The spread of those replays is the forecast range.

18
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:07,889
Here it is in action.

19
00:01:07,889 --> 00:01:10,358
Offers reprice, drop off, or come back.

20
00:01:10,358 --> 00:01:14,790
Each replay gives a new median, and together, they build the forecast.

21
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,652
On top of that, we have a crew of five AI agents, each with one job.

22
00:01:19,652 --> 00:01:25,397
A list reader, a repricer watcher, a base-rate keeper, a reference weigher, and a jump spotter.

23
00:01:25,397 --> 00:01:27,030
We average their forecasts.

24
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:29,281
Now, the recursive part.

25
00:01:29,281 --> 00:01:34,096
In training, the crew forecasts some practice days, and a verifier scores it.

26
00:01:34,096 --> 00:01:36,348
Then a coach AI proposes one change.

27
00:01:36,348 --> 00:01:43,102
A second verifier keeps that change only if practice days get better, and hidden check days don't get worse.

28
00:01:43,102 --> 00:01:44,916
The verifiers are fixed code.

29
00:01:44,916 --> 00:01:46,416
The AI can't touch them.

30
00:01:46,416 --> 00:01:51,982
After ten rounds, a final exam on unseen days: four percent better than where we started.

31
00:01:52,732 --> 00:01:55,110
At inference time, the crew is frozen.

32
00:01:55,110 --> 00:02:01,180
For each price, the five agents read the facts, we average their answers, and stretch the ranges.

33
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:04,809
One pass per price, a hundred and eleven forecasts in all.

34
00:02:05,559 --> 00:02:08,785
And we wrote the test down before we had any results.

35
00:02:08,785 --> 00:02:10,063
A committed protocol.

36
00:02:10,063 --> 00:02:12,437
A time machine that only sees the past.

37
00:02:12,437 --> 00:02:14,080
Shuffled data as a placebo.

38
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:16,027
And exam days no model had seen.

39
00:02:16,777 --> 00:02:21,052
Across three separate tests, the engine beat drift by about eight percent.

40
00:02:21,052 --> 00:02:23,767
The five agents beat it by fifteen to nineteen.

41
00:02:24,517 --> 00:02:26,120
But not every idea worked.

42
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:29,450
We tried seven ways to make the system improve itself.

43
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:34,000
Two held up: the coach, and reinforcement learning that teaches a small model to forecast.

44
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,802
Three didn't.

45
00:02:35,802 --> 00:02:37,775
And two, we just can't call yet.

46
00:02:38,525 --> 00:02:39,530
One honest caveat.

47
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:42,934
Our ranges held the truth eighty percent of the time overall.

48
00:02:42,934 --> 00:02:46,673
But when the price actually moved, that drops to sixty-six percent.

49
00:02:47,423 --> 00:02:49,182
So, three things to remember.

50
00:02:49,182 --> 00:02:50,456
Look inside the list.

51
00:02:50,456 --> 00:02:55,067
A crew of AI agents plus a coach cut error by twenty percent on unseen days.

52
00:02:55,067 --> 00:02:58,040
And most self-improvement ideas fail a fair test.

53
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:01,134
The verifier is what makes the loop worth trusting.

54
00:03:01,134 --> 00:03:02,347
Thanks for watching.
